# KataGo Inference Compatibility — Plan

## Goal

Add a runtime-dispatched code path so MiniGo's existing C++ runtime
(MCTS + NNEvaluator + AsyncBot + main_play / main_benchmark / main_evaluate)
can run an exported KataGo neural network at full strength on the
**TensorRT backend only**, while leaving the existing MiniGo `.onnx`
pipeline byte-for-byte unchanged.

Scope is intentionally narrow:
- **Inference only.** No training on KataGo weights. No selfplay-data
  format change. No Python-side changes to `scripts/`.
- **TensorRT backend only.** Eigen / CUDA / OpenCL / Metal / RKNN are not
  touched and will throw "unsupported model format" if pointed at a
  KataGo ONNX. They keep working unmodified for MiniGo ONNX.
- **One binary, one build.** Same `cmake -DMINIGO_BACKEND=tensorrt ..`
  produces `minigo_play`, `minigo_benchmark`, `minigo_evaluate`
  binaries that handle either format. The format is detected when the
  ONNX is loaded; no `#ifdef KATAGO`, no second build target, no
  duplicate engine cache directory.
- **No new dependencies.** Runtime needs only what MiniGo already
  links: TensorRT, CUDA, the in-tree minimal ONNX parser. KataGo's
  C++ source tree is read for reference (input encoder port) but not
  linked.

The driving use case is "play kata1 b10c128 (or any KataGo network) in
MiniGo's MCTS so we can run apples-to-apples evaluation matches against
MiniGo candidates without launching two engines and shuttling JSON over
GTP."


## Core principle: KataGo weights are inference-only

This is the spine of the plan. Every design decision and every diff
should be measured against it.

**KataGo weights enter the system through `main_play`,
`main_benchmark`, and `main_evaluate` only.** They drive MCTS, they
emit moves, they produce policy/value/score/ownership for analysis.
Nothing else.

**KataGo weights never become training data, never get fine-tuned,
never write selfplay records, never feed the Python trainer.** The
training pipeline (`scripts/train.py`, `scripts/train_continuous.py`,
`scripts/run_continuous.py`, `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` as a
warm-init source) sees no KataGo runtime path. The selfplay binary
rejects KataGo models with a clear error before any encoding work
happens.

This boundary lets us answer every "should X support KataGo?"
question by checking which side of the boundary X is on:

| Component | KataGo-aware? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `main_play` | yes | inference for human/GTP play |
| `main_benchmark` | yes | throughput measurement |
| `main_evaluate` | yes | match games (the driving use case) |
| `main_selfplay` | **no** — error early | writes training data; out of scope |
| `scripts/train*.py`, `run_continuous.py` | **no** — unchanged | training pipeline |
| `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` | additive parser extension only; CLI behavior + warm-init output unchanged (validated by byte-diff `training.pt`) | parser is shared with the converter |
| MCTS | format-agnostic at the algorithm level; 3 call-site renames on inference paths, selfplay encode at `:699` deliberately left as `game.encode()` for defense-in-depth | logic doesn't see weights, but call sites are the dispatch boundary |
| NNEvaluator queue, AsyncBot, ComputeContext | format-agnostic; no edits | true logic-only layers |
| TensorRT backend | yes | the inference path |
| Eigen / CUDA / OpenCL / Metal / RKNN | error early | unsupported, fail loudly |
| Selfplay V2 record format | unchanged | can't be written by KataGo path |

**Minimal-change discipline:** every edit to existing MiniGo files
must be (a) strictly additive (new fields, new methods, new branches
that only fire when `format == KataGo`) or (b) a five-line guard that
errors out on KataGo input. The MiniGo code paths must stay logically
identical — the same bytes flow through them whether or not KataGo
support exists in the binary.

Concrete consequences of this discipline:
- No renaming existing types, methods, or fields.
- No removing existing fields even if they look redundant.
- No reorganizing `GoGame`'s state, `BatchEvaluator`'s interface, or
  `NNResultBuf`'s layout — only additions.
- No changing the V2 record format, selfplay augmentation, or the
  Python trainer's input contract.
- The MiniGo ONNX flow must produce **byte-identical** output before
  vs. after this work — same ONNX, same engine cache hit, same
  policy/value, same self-play game seeds, same training runs.

Code review findings (P1/P2 from the recent review) fold into this
principle: each one was either a missed boundary violation (selfplay
shouldn't dispatch) or a missed minimal-change requirement (encode
belongs to the evaluator, not the search; recent-actions storage is
purely additive; `NNResultBuf` extension is purely additive).

Cross-reference of where each review finding is addressed:

| Finding | Resolution | Where in plan |
|---|---|---|
| P1: parser data discarded | Phase 0.1: extend `PolicyHead`/`ValueHead` dataclasses with `Optional` fields, populate in parsers (additive). Byte-diff `training.pt` to validate. | Phase 0.1 |
| P1: MCTS has no model handle | Encode dispatch moves to `BatchEvaluator::encode_state()` virtual; `NNEvaluator` overrides with format check; MCTS just calls the evaluator. | "Where the dispatch lives" + Phase 2 |
| P2: V7 history needs moves | Add `recent_actions_` private array + `recent_action()` accessor to `GoGame`. Maintained in `play()` / `reset()` / `copy()`. Strictly additive. | Phase 2.5 |
| P2: NNEvaluator drops score_sd / ownership (success path) | Add fields to `NNResultBuf`; copy through at server-thread writeback. Strictly additive (also a pre-existing latent MiniGo bug). | Phase 2.5 |
| P2: NNEvaluator catch path leaves new fields stale | Catch-path reset in `nn_evaluator.cpp:151-159` also clears `score_sd` and `ownership`. | Phase 2.5 |
| P2: selfplay dispatch contradicts non-goal | Drop `main_selfplay.cpp` from encoder-rename list; add early-error guard rejecting `format == KataGo`. | Phase 6 |
| P1: benchmark section 5 also crosses the boundary | Same boundary, finer guard: `main_benchmark.cpp` sections 1-4 are KataGo-aware; section 5 (self-play, builds TrainingRecords) is wrapped in a `format == MiniGo` `if/else` and skipped for KataGo. | Phase 6 |
| P1: TRT branch references missing `I.format` | Add `ModelFormat format` field to `TensorRTComputeHandle::Impl`; set from `model->format` in the ctor alongside existing `board_size`/`input_channels` assignments. | Phase 4 |
| P3: verification commands use wrong CLI flags | Phase 5 commands now match the actual `argv` parsing in each binary (`--sims`, `--max-batch`/`--nn-iters`, `--model1`/`--model2`). | Phase 5 |
| P1: benchmark sections 2-3 also encode | Rename `game.encode(state)` → `evaluator_->encode_state(...)` at `main_benchmark.cpp:165, 188` (direct + batched inference paths). Section 5 stays guarded. | Phase 2 |
| P2: selfplay rename weakens boundary | Drop `mcts.cpp:699` from rename list. Selfplay's encode stays as `game.encode()` so a KataGo evaluator cannot produce KataGo-shaped training records even if entry guards are bypassed. | Phase 2 |
| P2: pass normalization order | Insert recent-action push *after* `if (action == n*n) action = PASS_MOVE;` so passes record as `PASS_MOVE` (-1), not the pre-normalized `n*n` slot. | Phase 2.5 |
| P2: stale "byte-for-byte unchanged" parser claim | Updated `tools/katago_to_onnx.py` design section to say "additive parser extension" with reference to Phase 0.1 byte-diff verification. | "ONNX conversion" |
| P3: missing `Optional` import | Phase 0.1 prerequisite step adds `Optional` to `from typing import List, Union` → `List, Optional, Union`. | Phase 0.1 |
| P2: early "What's untouched" still claims `mcts.cpp` / `NNResultBuf` unchanged | Section retitled "What's logically unchanged for MiniGo" and rewritten to describe behavior preservation, not file untouchedness. Authoritative file list lives in "Files to add/modify". | "What's logically unchanged for MiniGo" |
| P3: component table row "warm_init_from_katago.py unchanged" | Row updated: "additive parser extension only; CLI behavior + warm-init output unchanged (validated by byte-diff `training.pt`)". | Component table |


## Source-backed alignment with KataGo

Design references KataGo's public source
(`github.com/lightvector/KataGo`).  The reference points are:

- Input encoding (V7): `cpp/neuralnet/nninputs.cpp`, `fillRowV7()`. 22
  spatial planes + 19 global features, documented in `nninputs.h`. This
  is the spec we port into `src/katago_inputs.cpp`. Treat KataGo's code
  as the authoritative reference; differences in our port are bugs.
- Network architecture: `cpp/neuralnet/desc.cpp` (binary format) +
  `cpp/neuralnet/openclbackend.cpp` / `cudabackend.cpp` (forward
  graph). The PyTorch `KataGoNet` (phase 0) mirrors this. Pre-act BN,
  gpool block channel split (`trunk_c=128 = regular_c=96 +
  gpool_c=32`), policy head's gpool injection into pass logit, value
  head pool order (mean+max+std).
- Output post-processing: `cpp/neuralnet/nninterface.cpp` /
  `cpp/neuralnet/nneval.cpp` show how the raw graph outputs map to
  `NNOutput` fields (softmax over value logits, tanh→[0,1] for
  ownership, `softplus` and `× 20` scaling on score mean/stdev,
  selecting policy channel 0 of 2). We bake all of this into our
  exported ONNX graph (phase 0) so the C++ side never has to
  replicate it.
- KataGo binary format versions v8-v15: the existing
  `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` already parses these. The phase 0
  refactor extracts that parser; no new format-version work needed.
  Newer v16+ formats would require updating the parser; tracked as a
  follow-up if/when needed.
- We deliberately do NOT depend on upstream KataGo's
  `python/export_onnx.py`. Our converter is self-contained in this
  repo's Python env. See "ONNX conversion" section below for
  rationale.

Where we deliberately deviate from KataGo it is because we are running
KataGo's network inside MiniGo's *MCTS*, not KataGo's. Details inline.


## Motivation

The existing `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` solves a different
problem: warm-init MiniGo's randomly-init trunk from KataGo's trained
weights so MiniGo's training converges faster. That tool transfers
~50% of params and discards the rest because MiniGo's architecture
differs (post-act BN, no gpool, simpler heads, 17-channel input vs
22+19, no per-position scoring or pass-via-gpool, etc.). The result is
a *MiniGo* model with a partial KataGo prior; it must still be trained.

What we want here is the opposite: take a fully trained KataGo network
and use it *unchanged* for inference. No training. No retraining. The
weights stay exactly as KataGo trained them. Only the runtime needs to
adapt.

Why bother:
1. **Reference strength.** kata1 b10c128 is publicly available and
   stronger than anything MiniGo can self-train in a week. Using it as
   the reference opponent in `main_evaluate` gives a stable Elo anchor
   independent of MiniGo's own training curve.
2. **Apples-to-apples MCTS.** Running KataGo's net through MiniGo's
   MCTS removes search-config differences when comparing nets head to
   head. A loss tells you "the net is weaker," not "the search was
   tuned differently."
3. **Debugging.** When MiniGo plays terribly, having a known-good net
   in the same C++ runtime lets you isolate "is it the search?" from
   "is it the net?" with a single binary swap.
4. **Future feature flag for distillation.** If we later choose
   option 3 (distillation, see initial discussion), we'll need to run
   KataGo as the teacher in-process anyway. This work is a strict
   prerequisite.


## Architecture: what differs, what doesn't

### Inputs

| | MiniGo | KataGo (V7) | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| # of input tensors | 1 | 2 | new branch in TRT bind |
| Spatial input | `state [N, 17, H, W]` | `input_spatial [N, 22, H, W]` | new encoder |
| Global input | n/a | `input_global [N, 19]` | new encoder |
| Spatial planes | 8 history × 2 colors + 1 color | stones, liberties (1/2/3+), ko, area, ladder, pass-alive, history | port `fillRowV7` |
| Global features | n/a | komi, rules, ko/scoring/encore state, suicide flag | port `fillRowV7` |

### Outputs

KataGo emits more heads than MiniGo. We only need the five MiniGo MCTS
already consumes: policy, value, scoreMean, scoreStdev, ownership.
Other KataGo heads (futurepos, seki, scoring, lead, vTime,
moremiscvalue, scorebelief logits) we **don't even include in the
exported ONNX** — our converter (see "ONNX conversion" below) only
builds the graph nodes for the heads we care about. No dummy bindings
needed in C++.

Because we own the converter, post-processing math is baked into the
ONNX graph. KataGo and MiniGo ONNX produce identically-shaped,
identically-scaled outputs:

| Output | MiniGo ONNX | KataGo ONNX (our export) |
|---|---|---|
| Policy | `policy_logits [N, action_size]` | `policy_logits [N, action_size]` (channel-0 slice baked in) |
| Value | `value [N, 1]` (P(W) − P(L)) | `value [N, 1]` (softmax → diff baked in) |
| Score mean | `score_mean [N, 1]` (raw points) | `score_mean [N, 1]` (`× 20` baked in) |
| Score stdev | `score_stdev [N, 1]` (positive) | `score_stdev [N, 1]` (softplus + `× 20` baked in) |
| Ownership | `ownership [N, H*W]` (sigmoid → [0,1]) | `ownership [N, H*W]` (`(tanh+1)/2` baked in) |
| Pass slot | last index | last index |

Net effect: the C++ TRT branch differs from the MiniGo path only on
the input side (split flat state into 2 device buffers, set 2 input
shapes, bind 2 input addresses). Output binding and result unpacking
is identical to MiniGo's path — same tensor names, same shapes, same
post-processing (none, because the graph already did it).

The policy ordering — board² spatial entries followed by 1 pass entry
— is the same in both formats, by convention. Verify on the first run
by checking that an empty-board pass logit is sensibly low; if it's
the largest entry, the slot ordering is reversed and we adjust at
export time (slice differently in `KataGoPolicyHead.forward`).

### Action ordering inside the spatial portion of policy

MiniGo: row-major `[r * W + c]`, top-to-bottom, left-to-right.
KataGo: same — verified by reading `nnoutput.cpp:posToLoc`. ✓

### What's logically unchanged for MiniGo

This section describes which behaviors stay byte-identical for MiniGo
runs, not which files stay untouched on disk. The authoritative
file-by-file list is in "Files to add/modify" near the bottom of this
plan.

**Behaviors preserved verbatim for MiniGo:**
- MCTS search algorithm: the descend / evaluate / expand / backprop
  loop, virtual-loss handling, root noise, temperature, tree reuse —
  all unchanged. The only edits to `mcts.cpp` are 3 call-site
  renames (`game.encode(state)` → `evaluator_->encode_state(...)`)
  on inference paths, plus *no* change to the `self_play_game_impl`
  encode at line 699.
- NNEvaluator server-thread loop, queue draining, condvar pattern,
  shutdown semantics — unchanged. The only edits are additive: an
  `encode_state` override and copy-through of two more fields in the
  per-buf writeback (and matching reset on the catch path).
- `NNResultBuf` request protocol: existing fields (`state_data`,
  `state_size`, `policy`, `value`, `score`, `mu`, `cv`, `done`)
  retain their meanings and lifetimes. Two additive fields
  (`score_sd`, `ownership`) appear at the end of the struct;
  existing callers don't read them. The KataGo path packs both
  spatial and global into the same flat `state` byte array, so
  `state_data` / `state_size` semantics are preserved.
- AsyncBot, NNRequestQueue, ComputeContext interface — no edits
  whatsoever.
- Selfplay V2 record format, augmentation (`mcts.cpp:608-668`),
  `self_play_game_impl` — no edits. Selfplay rejects KataGo at
  `main_selfplay.cpp` entry, so these paths are MiniGo-only by
  construction.

The TRT handle splits the flat `state` back into the two TRT inputs
at `predict_batch` time. The search-thread interface stays
identical.


## Design choice: runtime dispatch, not compile-time

We dispatch on `model->format` — a field set when `LoadedModel::load()`
parses the ONNX — rather than `#ifdef KATAGO_FORMAT`.

Justification:

1. **The TRT backend already does runtime dispatch by another name.**
   `src/tensorrt_compute.cpp:484-509` walks the engine's I/O tensors
   and matches them by string name (`"policy_logits"`, `"value"`,
   `"score_mean"`, `"ownership"`, …) to assign device buffer
   pointers. It even has a shape-based fallback for unknown names.
   Adding KataGo's tensor names to the same match table is the same
   pattern, not a new one.
2. **Same binary handles both formats.** `main_evaluate model_a.onnx
   model_b.onnx` can match KataGo against MiniGo in one process. With
   `#ifdef` you would need IPC.
3. **No build matrix doubling.** No `_katago` / `_minigo` binary
   suffix proliferation. CI doesn't double. Engine cache key already
   includes the model path, so KataGo and MiniGo engines never
   collide.
4. **The branch is cold.** One `if (model->format == KataGo)` per
   `predict_batch` call (i.e. once per ~256-state GPU batch). Cost is
   unmeasurable.
5. **The KataGo-only code is bounded.** ~700 LoC encoder (cold during
   MiniGo runs) plus ~80 LoC of TRT branching. Carrying that in the
   MiniGo build costs nothing.

`#ifdef` would only be the right call if KataGo support pulled in heavy
new dependencies. It does not — both paths use the same TRT, CUDA, and
in-tree ONNX parser.


## Where the dispatch lives: evaluator-side, not call-site

The encode dispatch belongs to the evaluator, not the search. This is
both the right OO answer and what minimal-change discipline forces.

**Why not call-site dispatch.** An earlier draft of this plan put the
`if (model->format == KataGo)` check at every `game.encode(state)`
call site (`mcts.cpp:248, 340, 413, 699`, plus root-eval call sites
in the four `main_*.cpp` files). That requires every caller to hold a
`LoadedModel*`. MCTS doesn't hold one — `mcts.h:100,156` shows it
owns `BatchEvaluator* evaluator_` and `const Config&`. Pushing
`format` into `Config` doesn't work either: `main_evaluate.cpp:221`
builds **one** `Config` from `model1->input_channels` and shares it
across both MCTS instances, so a single `format` field would be
ambiguous in cross-format matches. The whole approach is wrong.

**Why evaluator-side dispatch is right.** The `BatchEvaluator`
interface is exactly the layer that knows about the model — its
`NNEvaluator` subclass already owns `std::shared_ptr<LoadedModel>`.
Every MCTS instance gets its own evaluator. The dispatch fits there
naturally:

```cpp
// include/batch_evaluator.h — additive change
class BatchEvaluator {
public:
    virtual ~BatchEvaluator() = default;
    using Result = NNOutput;

    // NEW: encode a game position into the format this evaluator's
    // model expects. Default: MiniGo's 17-channel encoder.
    virtual void encode_state(const GoGame& game,
                              std::vector<float>& out) const {
        game.encode(out);
    }

    /* existing methods unchanged */
};
```

```cpp
// src/nn_evaluator.cpp — single override
void NNEvaluator::encode_state(const GoGame& game,
                               std::vector<float>& out) const {
    if (model_->format == ModelFormat::KataGo) {
        encode_for_katago(game, model_.get(), out);
    } else {
        game.encode(out);
    }
}
```

Search-side change is one rename per call site:
```cpp
// src/mcts.cpp:248, 340, 413, 699 (and same pattern in main_*.cpp)
- game_copy_ptr->encode(state);
+ evaluator_->encode_state(*game_copy_ptr, state);
```

This is ~10 changed lines total in MCTS + four `main_*.cpp` files.
MCTS itself becomes format-agnostic — it doesn't import
`loaded_model.h`, doesn't see `ModelFormat`, doesn't hold a model
pointer. The evaluator hides the format question entirely.

**Bonus: this fixes a pre-existing latent bug.** `main_evaluate.cpp`
currently uses one `Config.input_channels` (taken from `model1`) for
both MCTS instances. If the two models had different `input_channels`
(e.g., a future MiniGo with 19 channels vs the current 17), the
encoded states for `mcts_2` would be sized wrong and crash. With
evaluator-side dispatch each evaluator encodes for its own model.
The `Config` field becomes architectural metadata only and can be
deprecated.


## Out of scope (explicit non-goals)

- Training on KataGo weights, fine-tuning KataGo, or producing
  KataGo-format checkpoints from MiniGo selfplay. Use
  `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` if you want the warm-init flow.
- Backends other than TensorRT. Eigen / CUDA / OpenCL / Metal / RKNN
  remain MiniGo-only. They will report "unsupported model format" if
  given a KataGo ONNX and exit cleanly.
- Selfplay using KataGo's net to write MiniGo selfplay records. The
  selfplay binary is pointed at MiniGo's ONNX as today; if anyone
  later wants KataGo as the teacher for distillation, that's a
  follow-up that needs target-format work too.
- KataGo configuration knobs that change the input encoding
  (different rules sets beyond Tromp-Taylor / Japanese, encore
  phases, button go, handicap, multi-stone-suicide). The port covers
  the most-common case (area scoring, fixed komi, no encore). Other
  modes hit `assert(false)` until someone needs them.
- Loading KataGo's native `.bin.gz` / `.txt.gz` directly without ONNX
  conversion. We require an exported ONNX as input, produced once by
  our in-tree `tools/katago_to_onnx.py` (see "ONNX conversion"
  section).
- BFloat16 / FP8 calibration tuning specific to KataGo. The existing
  TRT precision auto-detection (BF16 on SM 8.0+, FP16 on Volta/Turing,
  FP32 on older) just works. If KataGo's score head needs different
  precision it's a follow-up.


## ONNX conversion: in-tree tool, not upstream's export

Step zero is converting KataGo's `.bin.gz` / `.txt.gz` to ONNX. We do
this with our own tool rather than upstream KataGo's
`python/export_onnx.py`. Three reasons:

1. **The binary parser already lives in this repo.**
   `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` contains ~600 lines of pure-Python
   `.bin.gz` / `.txt.gz` parser (port of KataGo's
   `cpp/neuralnet/desc.cpp`). It already reads `ConvLayer`,
   `BatchNormLayer`, `ActivationLayer`, `MatMulLayer`,
   `ResidualBlock`, `GPoolBlock`, `PolicyHead`, `ValueHead`. This code
   is currently used only by the warm-init flow; reusing it for ONNX
   conversion costs one refactor and zero new parser code.

2. **Self-contained Python env.** Upstream's `export_onnx.py` requires
   cloning the KataGo repo, installing its `requirements.txt`
   (different torch versions, occasionally incompatible CUDA pins),
   and tracking upstream API churn. An in-tree converter uses
   MiniGo's existing Python env — no extra setup, no version drift,
   reproducible alongside the rest of `scripts/`.

3. **We own the ONNX shape, which collapses C++ divergence.** Because
   we control the export, we choose I/O names and bake post-processing
   into the graph. We make the KataGo ONNX *look almost identical* to
   MiniGo's ONNX on the output side. This is the big win:

   | Output | Upstream export (we'd post-process in C++) | Our export (post-processing baked into graph) |
   |---|---|---|
   | Policy | `output_policy [N, 2, A]` — take ch 0 in C++ | `policy_logits [N, A]` — slice in graph |
   | Value | `output_value [N, 3]` — softmax+diff in C++ | `value [N, 1]` — softmax+diff in graph |
   | ScoreMean | `output_miscvalue [N, ~10]` — slice idx 0, `×20` in C++ | `score_mean [N, 1]` — `Mul ×20` in graph |
   | ScoreStdev | same — slice idx 1, softplus, `×20` | `score_stdev [N, 1]` — softplus + `Mul` in graph |
   | Ownership | `output_ownership [N,1,H,W]` — tanh→sigmoid in C++ | `ownership [N, H*W]` — `(tanh+1)/2` in graph |

   The right column reduces the C++ TRT branch dramatically. The
   *only* divergence between MiniGo and KataGo paths in
   `tensorrt_compute.cpp` is "2 inputs vs 1." All output
   post-processing is identical.  The C++ "KataGo branch" shrinks
   from ~80 LoC to ~30 LoC. We also get to skip allocating
   `d_policy_2ch`, `d_value_3ch`, `d_miscvalue`, and the
   unused-output trash buffer entirely — those graph ops fold into
   TRT's optimized kernels at engine-build time.

The cost: ~400-600 LoC of PyTorch-side `KataGoNet` mirroring KataGo's
trunk + heads. We only implement the heads we consume (policy, value,
scoreMean, scoreStdev, ownership) — futurepos / seki / scoring / lead
/ scorebelief / vTime are skipped because MCTS doesn't read them.
Adding `torch.onnx.export` with the chosen I/O names is ~50 LoC on
top.

### `tools/katago_to_onnx.py` — design

**No refactor; additive parser extension only.**
`tools/warm_init_from_katago.py`'s CLI is gated by
`if __name__ == "__main__": main()`, so importing the module is
side-effect-free. We import the parser directly rather than splitting
it into a new file:

```python
# tools/katago_to_onnx.py
import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from warm_init_from_katago import (
    KataGoModel, parse_katago_model,
    ConvLayer, BatchNormLayer, ActivationLayer,
    MatMulLayer, MatBiasLayer,
    ResidualBlock, GPoolBlock, PolicyHead, ValueHead, Trunk,
)
```

The file is **not** byte-for-byte unchanged — Phase 0.1 extends its
`PolicyHead` and `ValueHead` dataclasses to retain the
previously-discarded head layers we need to build the inference
graph. The change is strictly additive (`Optional[...] = None`
fields plus a few populating lines in the parser bodies). The
warm-init code paths read only the original required fields, so a
byte-diff of `training.pt` validates equivalence (Phase 0
verification milestone).

Stylistic note: the file becomes both a CLI tool and a parser
library. That's a normal Python convention for any `.py` with a
`__main__` guard; a separate `katago_parser.py` would be marginally
cleaner but adds risk for no functional gain.

New `tools/katago_arch.py`: a PyTorch `nn.Module` that mirrors KataGo's
architecture **for inference only**. Components:

- `KataGoStem`: spatial input conv + global-input matmul (per
  upstream `Trunk::forward`, the global features get projected and
  added as a bias to the trunk channels at the input).
- `KataGoOrdinaryBlock`: pre-activation BN → conv → BN → conv +
  residual. Two BNs are pre-act (gamma/beta + running stats); we use
  `nn.BatchNorm2d` with `affine=True, track_running_stats=True` and
  load the running stats from KataGo's parsed values directly (no
  fold).
- `KataGoGPoolBlock`: regular branch (96 channels) + gpool branch (32
  channels mean+max → matmul → broadcast add to regular). All from
  upstream `cpp/neuralnet/openclbackend.cpp`'s description; the
  `warm_init_from_katago.py` already groks the layout.
- `KataGoPolicyHead`: g1Conv (gpool features) + p1Conv (spatial
  features) → spatial output + pass via FC. Output reshaped to `[N,
  H*W + 1]` matching MiniGo's policy slot ordering.
- `KataGoValueHead`: v1Conv → global mean+max+std pool → 3 FC layers
  → split outputs.
  - `value_logits → softmax → P(W) − P(L) → [N, 1]`: same shape as
    MiniGo's `value`.
  - `score_mean_raw → × 20 → [N, 1]`.
  - `score_stdev_raw → softplus → × 20 → [N, 1]`.
- `KataGoOwnershipHead`: separate conv from pre-pool features → tanh
  → `(x + 1) * 0.5` → reshape `[N, H*W]`.

Inputs:
- `state_spatial [N, 22, H, W]` (named to differ from MiniGo's
  `state` to avoid name collisions; the C++ side's two-input check
  works on count regardless).
- `state_global  [N, 19]`.

Outputs (chosen to mirror MiniGo names):
- `policy_logits [N, H*W + 1]`
- `value         [N, 1]`         already P(W) − P(L)
- `score_mean    [N, 1]`         already `× 20`
- `score_stdev   [N, 1]`         already softplus + `× 20`, positive
- `ownership     [N, H*W]`       already in `[0, 1]`

This matches MiniGo's existing tensor names exactly except for the
input names and the existence of a second input. The TRT name-match
table needs only one new entry per format-deciding input
(`state_spatial` / `state_global`).

`tools/katago_to_onnx.py` (~150 LoC): glue. CLI: `--katago-bin`,
`--board`, `--output`. Loads the binary, instantiates `KataGoNet`,
copies weights from parsed dataclasses to nn.Module parameters,
runs a forward pass for shape validation, calls `torch.onnx.export`
with `dynamic_axes={state_spatial: {0: "batch"}, state_global: {0:
"batch"}, ...}` so TRT can build a dynamic-batch engine.

Workflow:
```
wget https://media.katagotraining.org/uploaded/networks/models/kata1/kata1-b10c128-s1141046784-d204142634.bin.gz
python tools/katago_to_onnx.py \
    --katago-bin kata1-b10c128-s1141046784-d204142634.bin.gz \
    --board 19 --output models/kata1-b10c128.onnx
```

Verification: load the ONNX with `onnx.checker.check_model()`, then
run a single forward pass through both the PyTorch nn.Module and
ONNX Runtime on the same dummy input; assert outputs match within
1e-5. If they don't, the export has a bug — fail loudly.

### Architecture validation: parity test

Once the converter exists, we need a one-shot test that the
PyTorch-side `KataGoNet` is a faithful translation of KataGo's
runtime. Without this, all downstream debugging (encoder bugs vs net
bugs) becomes guesswork.

Add `tools/katago_parity_test.py`: takes a small set of board
positions in SGF or plain text, runs them through:
1. Upstream KataGo's `katago benchmark` or `katago analyze` (called
   as a subprocess if available; skip with a warning if not),
2. Our converted ONNX via ONNX Runtime,

and compares policy + value + score outputs. Tolerances ~1e-3
(post-softmax for value, top-N cosine for policy). Disagreement is a
bug in our PyTorch architecture or weight copy — fix before
proceeding.

If upstream KataGo isn't installed, the parity test reduces to
"PyTorch nn.Module vs ONNX Runtime self-consistency", which catches
ONNX export bugs but not architecture bugs. That's still useful as a
smoke test.


## Implementation phases

Each phase is independently testable. After each phase the existing
MiniGo `.onnx` flow still works exactly as before. Order matters:
phases 0-4 are sequential dependencies; phase 5 is integration; phase
6 is hardening.

### Phase 0: ONNX converter (Python only, no C++ touched)

Goal: produce a working KataGo ONNX file using only this repo's
Python env. Decouples the C++ work from "is upstream's exporter
available."

Steps:

0.1. **Extend the parser in `tools/warm_init_from_katago.py` to
retain all head layers (additive only).** The existing parser
intentionally drops most policy/value head layers — `_parse_policy_head`
keeps only `p1_conv` and `p1_bn` (lines 491-513), and
`_parse_value_head` keeps only `v1_conv` and `v1_bn` (lines 516-534).
Everything else is `parse()`'d for stream-position consistency and
discarded. Warm-init only needs those four layers; the full inference
graph needs all of them.

The fix is purely additive to the dataclasses and parsers.

**Prerequisite: typing import.**
`warm_init_from_katago.py:79` currently has `from typing import List,
Union`. The new `Optional[...]` annotations need:
```python
from typing import List, Optional, Union   # add Optional
```
A one-token addition. Without it, the module fails at import time
with `NameError: name 'Optional' is not defined` — caught immediately
by the warm-init byte-diff test, but worth doing first to keep the
diff clean.

Then the dataclass extensions:

```python
# PolicyHead dataclass — add Optional fields for everything currently
# parsed-and-dropped. Defaults to None so existing call sites that only
# touch p1_conv / p1_bn keep working unchanged.
@dataclass
class PolicyHead:
    name: str
    p1_conv: ConvLayer
    p1_bn:   BatchNormLayer
    g1_conv:        Optional[ConvLayer]      = None  # NEW
    g1_bn:          Optional[BatchNormLayer] = None  # NEW
    g1_act:         Optional[ActivationLayer] = None  # NEW
    gpool_to_bias:  Optional[MatMulLayer]    = None  # NEW
    p1_act:         Optional[ActivationLayer] = None  # NEW
    p2_conv:        Optional[ConvLayer]      = None  # NEW
    gpool_to_pass:  Optional[MatMulLayer]    = None  # NEW
    # v15+ extras (still Optional):
    gpool_to_pass_bias:  Optional[MatBiasLayer]    = None
    pass_act:            Optional[ActivationLayer] = None
    gpool_to_pass_mul2:  Optional[MatMulLayer]     = None

@dataclass
class ValueHead:
    name: str
    v1_conv: ConvLayer
    v1_bn:   BatchNormLayer
    v1_act:        Optional[ActivationLayer] = None  # NEW
    v2_mul:        Optional[MatMulLayer]     = None  # NEW
    v2_bias:       Optional[MatBiasLayer]    = None  # NEW
    v2_act:        Optional[ActivationLayer] = None  # NEW
    v3_mul:        Optional[MatMulLayer]     = None  # NEW
    v3_bias:       Optional[MatBiasLayer]    = None  # NEW
    sv3_mul:       Optional[MatMulLayer]     = None  # NEW
    sv3_bias:      Optional[MatBiasLayer]    = None  # NEW
    v_ownership_conv: Optional[ConvLayer]    = None  # NEW
```

Update `_parse_policy_head` and `_parse_value_head` to populate the
new fields instead of discarding the parsed objects:

```python
def _parse_policy_head(r, model_version, binary):
    name        = r.read_token()
    p1_conv     = ConvLayer.parse(r, binary)
    g1_conv     = ConvLayer.parse(r, binary)            # was discarded
    g1_bn       = BatchNormLayer.parse(r, binary)       # was discarded
    g1_act      = ActivationLayer.parse(r, model_version)
    gpool_bias  = MatMulLayer.parse(r, binary)
    p1_bn       = BatchNormLayer.parse(r, binary)
    p1_act      = ActivationLayer.parse(r, model_version)
    p2_conv     = ConvLayer.parse(r, binary)
    gpool_pass  = MatMulLayer.parse(r, binary)
    extras = {}
    if model_version >= 15:
        extras['gpool_to_pass_bias'] = MatBiasLayer.parse(r, binary)
        extras['pass_act']           = ActivationLayer.parse(r, model_version)
        extras['gpool_to_pass_mul2'] = MatMulLayer.parse(r, binary)
    return PolicyHead(name=name, p1_conv=p1_conv, p1_bn=p1_bn,
                      g1_conv=g1_conv, g1_bn=g1_bn, g1_act=g1_act,
                      gpool_to_bias=gpool_bias, p1_act=p1_act,
                      p2_conv=p2_conv, gpool_to_pass=gpool_pass, **extras)
```

(Same shape for `_parse_value_head`.)

This change is **strictly additive**:
- The required fields (`p1_conv`, `p1_bn`, `v1_conv`, `v1_bn`) remain
  required and unchanged — warm-init uses them by name and gets the
  same values it always got.
- The new fields are all `Optional[...] = None` and default to filled
  parsed values. Code that doesn't access them sees no difference.
- The `KataGoModel` overall structure, the `parse_katago_model`
  function signature, and warm-init call sites are unchanged.

Verification (mandatory before phase 0.2):
- Run warm-init on a known `.bin.gz` before this change → save
  resulting `training.pt` as `training_pre.pt`.
- Apply this change.
- Run warm-init on the same `.bin.gz` → save as `training_post.pt`.
- `python -c "import torch; a=torch.load('training_pre.pt'); b=torch.load('training_post.pt'); \
   for k in a['model_state_dict']: assert torch.equal(a['model_state_dict'][k], b['model_state_dict'][k]), k; \
   print('warm-init unchanged')"`
- If any tensor differs, the parser change is not actually additive
  — find why (likely an order-of-parse bug or accidental field
  access) before continuing.

Then `katago_to_onnx.py` imports the now-complete parser:
```python
from warm_init_from_katago import (
    KataGoModel, parse_katago_model,
    ConvLayer, BatchNormLayer, ActivationLayer,
    MatMulLayer, MatBiasLayer,
    ResidualBlock, GPoolBlock, PolicyHead, ValueHead, Trunk,
)
```

0.2. New file `tools/katago_arch.py`: PyTorch nn.Module mirroring
KataGo's runtime, inference-only. Components (per "ONNX conversion"
section above): `KataGoStem`, `KataGoOrdinaryBlock`,
`KataGoGPoolBlock`, `KataGoPolicyHead`, `KataGoValueHead`,
`KataGoOwnershipHead`, top-level `KataGoNet`.
- Pre-activation BN: `nn.BatchNorm2d` placed *before* each conv in
  the residual block (opposite of MiniGo's post-act). Running stats
  loaded directly from KataGo's parsed mean/var; gamma/beta from
  parsed scale/bias.
- GPool block channel split: the block input is `trunk_c=128`; it
  splits into a `regular_c=96` branch (3×3 conv) and a
  `gpool_c=32` branch (3×3 conv → mean+max global pool → matmul →
  per-channel bias added to regular branch). Combined output goes
  through a final 3×3 conv back to `trunk_c=128`. Layout per
  upstream `cpp/neuralnet/desc.cpp`.
- Heads bake post-processing: see "ONNX conversion" section.

0.3. New file `tools/katago_to_onnx.py` (~150 LoC):
- CLI: `--katago-bin <path>`, `--board <int>`, `--output <path>`,
  `--max-batch <int default 256>` (used only as a hint for the
  optimization profile — TRT side overrides anyway).
- Calls `parse_katago_model` to get a `KataGoModel`, instantiates
  `KataGoNet` with matching `(filters, blocks, board)`, copies
  weights from the parsed dataclasses to the nn.Module's
  `state_dict`, runs one forward pass on dummy input
  (`state_spatial = torch.zeros(1, 22, B, B); state_global =
  torch.zeros(1, 19)`), then `torch.onnx.export` with
  `input_names=["state_spatial", "state_global"]`,
  `output_names=["policy_logits", "value", "score_mean",
  "score_stdev", "ownership"]`, `dynamic_axes={...: {0: "batch"}}`.
- Asserts each named output's shape matches the table in "Outputs"
  above.

0.4. New file `tools/katago_parity_test.py` (~100 LoC):
- Runs the converted ONNX through ONNX Runtime on a small set of
  hand-picked positions (empty board, mid-game, near-endgame).
- If `katago` binary is on PATH: also runs `katago benchmark` or
  similar against the same positions and compares policy top-5
  cosine similarity (≥ 0.999) and value (`|Δ| ≤ 1e-3`).
- If `katago` is unavailable: reduces to a self-consistency check
  (PyTorch nn.Module vs ONNX Runtime); still catches export bugs.

Phase 0 result: `models/kata1-b10c128.onnx` exists. Loading it via
ONNX Runtime gives sensible policy + value on a few test positions.
No C++ changes yet. The MiniGo binary still has no idea KataGo
exists.

### Phase 1: format detection and metadata

`include/loaded_model.h`:
- Add `enum class ModelFormat { MiniGo, KataGo }` with default `MiniGo`.
- Add to `LoadedModel`:
  - `ModelFormat format = ModelFormat::MiniGo;`
  - `int input_global_channels = 0;`     // 0 for MiniGo, 19 for KataGo

That's it. No `policy_num_channels`, no `miscvalue_channels`, no
score-index fields — those don't exist in our exported KataGo ONNX
because the heads come out already in MiniGo shape.

`src/loaded_model.cpp`:
- Open the ONNX once, read graph **inputs** (not just initializers).
  If graph inputs include `state_spatial` and `state_global`, set
  `format = KataGo`. Else continue with the existing detection
  (`is_vit`, `is_new_resnet`).
- KataGo arm:
  - `input_channels = 22` (read from `state_spatial` shape dim 1)
  - `input_global_channels = 19` (read from `state_global` shape dim 1)
  - `board_size` from `state_spatial` H/W
  - Skip `load_conv` / `load_bn` / `load_fc` — TRT parses ONNX
    directly, no per-op weight extraction needed.
- Print line: `Model loaded: type=katago board=N channels=22+19`.

`include/onnx_loader.h` / `src/onnx_loader.cpp`:
- Add a helper to enumerate graph inputs:
  `std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::vector<int64_t>>> parse_onnx_graph_inputs(const std::string& path);`
  (Currently the parser only enumerates initializers.)
- Implementation: extend the minimal protobuf parser to walk the
  `graph.input[]` field as well. ~30 LoC. Don't pull in the real
  protobuf library.

Test: load a MiniGo ONNX and a KataGo ONNX, assert
`model->format` is set correctly for each. Existing MiniGo loads
remain unchanged in observable output.

### Phase 2: evaluator-side encode dispatch + scaffolding

Goal: route every encode through `BatchEvaluator::encode_state(...)`
without yet implementing the V7 encoder (phase 3). MCTS becomes
format-agnostic; format awareness is contained inside `NNEvaluator`.

`include/batch_evaluator.h` (additive):
- Add `virtual void encode_state(const GoGame&, std::vector<float>&) const`
  with default body `game.encode(out)` (preserves MiniGo behavior for
  any subclass that doesn't override).

`include/nn_evaluator.h` / `src/nn_evaluator.cpp` (additive):
- Override `encode_state` to call `encode_for_katago(...)` when
  `model_->format == ModelFormat::KataGo`, else `game.encode(out)`.

Call-site rename — only paths that the KataGo entry points
(`main_play`, `main_benchmark` sections 1-4, `main_evaluate`) can
actually reach. **Anything that builds training records or runs
self-play stays on `game.encode()`** as defense-in-depth: the entry
guards already reject KataGo, but leaving those call sites unchanged
ensures no future caller accidentally feeds a KataGo flat state into
augmentation or the V2 record writer.

- `src/mcts.cpp:248, 340, 413` — search-thread leaf evaluation paths
  reachable from `mcts.get_action()`. Rename
  `game_copy_ptr->encode(state)` → `evaluator_->encode_state(*game_copy_ptr, state)`.
- `src/mcts.cpp:699` — **no rename**. This is inside
  `self_play_game_impl`, building a per-step `state` for the V2
  training record. Augmentation (`mcts.cpp:608-668`) and the V2
  record writer assume `state.size() == input_channels * board_area`
  — a layout that exists only for MiniGo. The selfplay entry guard
  in `main_selfplay.cpp` (Phase 6) already prevents KataGo from
  reaching this function, but leaving `game.encode(step.state)` as
  hardcoded MiniGo encoding is a second layer of safety.
- `src/main_play.cpp`, `src/main_evaluate.cpp` root-eval call sites:
  rename. Both are inference paths.
- `src/main_benchmark.cpp` — multiple call sites need attention:
  - **Sections 2 & 3** (direct inference + batched eval throughput):
    `game.encode(state)` at lines 165 and 188 → rename. These feed
    `eval_single->evaluate_single` / `eval_single->evaluate`, which
    are inference paths and must work on KataGo.
  - **Section 4** (MCTS root eval): rename if a `game.encode()` call
    is present.
  - **Section 5** (multi-threaded self-play via `self_play_game()`):
    do not rename anything inside this section. The Phase 6
    benchmark guard skips section 5 entirely for KataGo, mirroring
    the selfplay guard.
- `src/main_selfplay.cpp`: **no rename**. The Phase 6 selfplay guard
  rejects `format == KataGo` before any encoding happens, and
  leaving the calls as `game.encode()` keeps the MiniGo-only
  invariant explicit at the call site.

`include/katago_inputs.h` (new file):
- Declare `void encode_for_katago(const GoGame& game, const LoadedModel* model, std::vector<float>& out);`
- Layout contract: `out` is sized to `(22 * H * W) + 19` floats;
  first block is spatial in row-major `[C, H, W]`; second block is
  global vector. The TRT handle assumes this layout when it splits
  the input back into the two TRT inputs.

`src/katago_inputs.cpp` (new file, phase 2 stub):
- Implements `encode_for_katago` as a stub that throws
  `std::runtime_error("KataGo encoder not yet implemented")`. Phase 3
  replaces it.

Phase 2 result: MiniGo runs still work (default
`encode_state` → `game.encode(...)`, byte-identical). A KataGo run
loads the model, dispatches into the override, and crashes at the
stub — expected, signals "phase 3 is the next gate."

`CMakeLists.txt`: add `src/katago_inputs.cpp` to the source list.

### Phase 2.5: extend `NNResultBuf` and `GoGame` (additive only)

Two small, strictly-additive changes that the V7 encoder and the
KataGo path will need. Both also clean up pre-existing latent bugs
in MiniGo.

**A. `NNResultBuf` carries `score_sd` and `ownership` through the
queue.** Today the buf has only `policy`/`value`/`score`
(`include/batch_evaluator.h:18-34`); the server thread at
`src/nn_evaluator.cpp:169-170` only copies `policy` and `value` from
the `predict_batch` result, dropping `score_sd` and `ownership`
before MCTS sees them. This is independently a bug for MiniGo —
TRT computes those fields but they're discarded en route. Fix:

```cpp
// include/batch_evaluator.h — additive
struct NNResultBuf {
    /* existing fields */
    float              score_sd = 0.0f;   // NEW
    std::vector<float> ownership;          // NEW
};
```

```cpp
// src/nn_evaluator.cpp:169-170 — add 2 lines on the success path
buf->policy    = std::move(all_results[i].policy);
buf->value     = all_results[i].value;
buf->score     = all_results[i].score;     // NEW (currently dropped)
buf->score_sd  = all_results[i].score_sd;  // NEW
buf->ownership = std::move(all_results[i].ownership);  // NEW
```

**Also clear the new fields on the failure path.**
`src/nn_evaluator.cpp:151-159` catches `predict_batch` exceptions
and resets `policy`/`value`/`score` on the affected bufs so search
threads don't deadlock or read garbage. Once `score_sd` and
`ownership` exist, the same catch path must clear them too —
otherwise a buf that had a successful eval, then a failed one,
would return stale ownership/uncertainty from the prior call:

```cpp
// src/nn_evaluator.cpp catch path — add 2 lines
int board_area = model_->board_size * model_->board_size;
for (auto* buf : batch) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(buf->mu);
    buf->policy.assign(action_size, 0.0f);
    buf->value     = 0.0f;
    buf->score     = 0.0f;
    buf->score_sd  = 0.0f;                            // NEW
    buf->ownership.assign(board_area, 0.0f);          // NEW
    buf->done      = true;
    buf->cv.notify_one();
}
```

And update the `evaluate_with_buf` return at `nn_evaluator.cpp:61`
to include all five fields. Any caller currently constructing an
`NNOutput` from the buf gets the same fields it already got, plus
two more — additive.

**B. `GoGame` records recent action locations.** V7's history planes
encode *where* the last few moves were played, not what the board
looked like (`game.cpp:241,260` shows MiniGo stores only board
snapshots in `ring_buf_`). Move locations cannot be reliably
recovered from snapshot diffs because (a) captures change multiple
cells, and (b) passes change zero cells. The fix is purely additive
to `GoGame`:

```cpp
// include/game.h — additive
class GoGame {
    // ... existing public state ...

    // NEW: most-recent action locations, [0] = 1 ply ago, ..., [4] = 5 plies ago.
    // Value range: [0, board_size²) for board moves, PASS_MOVE for pass,
    // -2 sentinel for "no move yet". Only used by the KataGo V7 encoder.
    int recent_action(int steps_back) const;

private:
    static constexpr int RECENT_ACTIONS_CAP = 5;
    std::array<int, RECENT_ACTIONS_CAP> recent_actions_;
    int                                  recent_actions_count_ = 0;
};
```

```cpp
// src/game.cpp::play() — insert AFTER pass normalization, BEFORE
// any board mutation. The existing first line of play() is:
//     if (action == n * n) action = PASS_MOVE;
// We must record the *normalized* action so recent_action(i) returns
// PASS_MOVE (-1) for passes, not the pre-normalized n*n slot.
//
// Insert this block immediately after that normalization line and
// before `std::memcpy(prev_board, board, ...)`:
for (int i = RECENT_ACTIONS_CAP - 1; i > 0; --i)
    recent_actions_[i] = recent_actions_[i - 1];
recent_actions_[0] = action;   // already normalized to PASS_MOVE for passes
if (recent_actions_count_ < RECENT_ACTIONS_CAP) ++recent_actions_count_;

// src/game.cpp::reset() — clear:
recent_actions_.fill(-2);
recent_actions_count_ = 0;

// src/game.cpp::copy() — propagate:
g.recent_actions_       = recent_actions_;
g.recent_actions_count_ = recent_actions_count_;
```

No public method changes, no existing field changes. Only `play()`,
`reset()`, and `copy()` get one block of additive lines each. MiniGo
code paths never touch `recent_actions_`, so behavior is byte-identical
for MiniGo runs.

Test: a unit test that plays a fixed move sequence (including a
pass via the unnormalized `n*n` slot, a pass via the explicit
`PASS_MOVE`, and a capture) and asserts `recent_action(0..4)`
returns `PASS_MOVE` for both pass forms and the right board
location for the capture. ~30 LoC.

### Phase 3: port `fillRowV7` (the long pole)

This is the bulk of the work. ~700 LoC of careful C++.

Reference: `KataGo/cpp/neuralnet/nninputs.cpp::NNInputs::fillRowV7`,
plus `nninputs.h` for plane numbering and `boardLogic.cpp` for
liberty / pass-alive / ladder helpers.

What `encode_for_katago` must compute, plane by plane:

Spatial (22 planes, all `[H, W]` float):
- `0`: on board (always 1 — for irregular boards it would mark valid)
- `1`: own stones at current move
- `2`: opp stones at current move
- `3`: own stones with 1 liberty
- `4`: own stones with 2 liberties
- `5`: own stones with 3+ liberties
- `6`: opp stones with 1 liberty
- `7`: opp stones with 2 liberties
- `8`: opp stones with 3+ liberties
- `9`: ko-banned point (simple ko, last move) — for our rules this is
  `last_move`'s recapture position if it would create immediate
  recapture
- `10`: own pass-alive areas (Benson)
- `11`: opp pass-alive areas
- `12`: ladder-escape moves (own; per upstream)
- `13`: ladder-capture moves (opp; per upstream)
- `14`-`16`: history (1, 2, 3 moves ago; binary "this point was just
  played by the player whose move it was at the time")
- `17`-`20`: more history / area / etc per upstream V7 layout
- `21`: misc / encore-specific (zeros for non-encore)

Confirm exact plane assignment from upstream — versions of `fillRowV7`
have shuffled planes 14-21 across releases. Use upstream's naming
constants as the source of truth. Don't trust this list.

Global (19 floats):
- `0`: komi (raw, often `7.5`)
- `1`-`4`: ko / suicide / scoring / encore-phase rule flags
- `5`-`12`: encoded bucketed komi, scoring, etc per upstream
- `13`-`18`: more rule flags / scoring policy

Confirm against upstream. Most flags are zero for area-scoring +
half-komi + Tromp-Taylor — our default rules.

What we need from `GoGame` to implement this that isn't already
exposed:
- Stones with N liberties: requires running `get_group()` /
  `get_group_on()` for every stone and grouping by liberty count.
  Expose internally; don't change the public `GoGame` API. Add a
  helper inside `katago_inputs.cpp` that takes
  `const GoGame&` and uses `game.board[][]` directly (already public).
- Pass-alive (Benson): port `nnboard.cpp::computePassAliveArea`. ~100
  LoC. This is the longest helper.
- Ladder detection: port `boardLogic.cpp::searchIsLadderCapturedAttackerFirst`.
  ~80 LoC, recursive with depth cap.
- History planes: use `recent_action(int steps_back)` from Phase
  2.5. Each history plane is a one-hot map: write `1.0` at the
  spatial index of `recent_action(i)` (or leave the plane all-zero
  if the action is `PASS_MOVE` or `-2`). MiniGo's snapshot ring
  buffer (`ring_buf_`) is **not** used by V7 — wrong representation
  for what V7 needs.

Optimizations:
- The encoder runs once per leaf node in MCTS. At ~256 states/batch,
  ~10k nodes/sec, encoder must be sub-100µs. The inner loops are
  O(H*W) for most planes, O(H*W*α) for liberties (small constant α
  via Union-Find or a per-call flood-fill cache), and O(H*W) for
  pass-alive (Benson is iterative). Total should be ~30-50µs on a 9×9
  board.
- Pre-allocate scratch buffers per-thread. The `NNResultBuf` already
  owns per-thread state; extend it with a scratch arena (or use
  `thread_local` inside the encoder file — simpler, still safe given
  one search thread per logical worker).

Tests (phase 3 alone, before TRT integration):
- `test_katago_inputs.cpp` — unit test that loads a known board
  position, encodes it, and compares the result byte-for-byte against
  a JSON dump produced by upstream KataGo's `python/encode_test.py`
  (or the C++ `runtests inputtest`). Use a handful of positions:
  empty board, mid-game, ladder, ko, pass-alive corner, near-end
  endgame. Save expected outputs as `tests/katago_v7_*.json`.
- A diff > 0.01 on any plane is a bug. Iterate until all positions
  match.

Phase 3 result: KataGo run still crashes at `predict_batch` (because
the TRT path isn't there yet) but the encoder is correct.

### Phase 4: TensorRT dual-input branch

The big win from owning the converter (phase 0) shows up here: the
output side is identical to MiniGo, so the only TRT divergence is
two inputs vs one. ~30 LoC of new code in `tensorrt_compute.cpp`.

`src/tensorrt_compute.cpp`:

Extend `TRTDeviceState` with the two KataGo input-tensor name fields:
```cpp
std::string input_spatial_name;      // KataGo: state_spatial
std::string input_global_name;       // KataGo: state_global
```

Extend the name-match loop (currently
`tensorrt_compute.cpp:484-509`) — but only the **input** half. The
output name set is unchanged (`policy_logits`, `value`, `score_mean`,
`score_stdev`, `ownership`):

```cpp
if (mode == nvinfer1::TensorIOMode::kINPUT) {
    if      (sname == "state_spatial") dev.input_spatial_name = name;
    else if (sname == "state_global")  dev.input_global_name  = name;
    else                                dev.input_name        = name;
}
```

If both `input_spatial_name` and `input_global_name` are set after
the loop, this is a KataGo engine; if `input_name` is set, it's
MiniGo. Cross-check against `model->format` and throw on mismatch.

Extend `TensorRTComputeHandle::Impl` with the format flag (so the
hot path branch in `predict_batch` knows which path to take) and
two KataGo input device buffers (only allocated when format is
KataGo):
```cpp
ModelFormat format = ModelFormat::MiniGo;  // copied from model in ctor
float* d_input_spatial = nullptr;          // KataGo: [max_batch, 22, H, W]
float* d_input_global  = nullptr;          // KataGo: [max_batch, 19]
```

The `format` field is set in `TensorRTComputeHandle`'s constructor
alongside the existing `board_size` / `input_channels` /
`max_batch_size` assignments (`tensorrt_compute.cpp:543-545`):
```cpp
I.format          = model->format;   // NEW
I.board_size      = model->board_size;
I.input_channels  = model->input_channels;
I.max_batch_size  = max_batch_size;
```

The output device buffers (`d_policy`, `d_value`, `d_score`,
`d_score_sd`, `d_ownership`) keep their existing semantics, sizes,
and code paths. Same shapes for both formats.

In `predict_batch`, the only KataGo-specific block is the input
section. The output read-back and `NNOutput` packing is unchanged
from the MiniGo path:

```cpp
if (I.format == ModelFormat::KataGo) {
    // Split each state vector into spatial + global parts.
    // Per encoder contract: each state is [22*H*W floats][19 floats].
    int sp_per = 22 * HW;
    int gl_per = 19;
    flat_spatial.assign(N * sp_per, 0.0f);
    flat_global.assign (N * gl_per, 0.0f);
    for (int n = 0; n < N; n++) {
        std::memcpy(&flat_spatial[n * sp_per], states[n].data(),         sp_per * 4);
        std::memcpy(&flat_global [n * gl_per], states[n].data() + sp_per, gl_per * 4);
    }
    cudaMemcpyAsync(I.d_input_spatial, flat_spatial.data(), ..., cudaStreamPerThread);
    cudaMemcpyAsync(I.d_input_global,  flat_global.data(),  ..., cudaStreamPerThread);

    exec_ctx->setInputShape(I.input_spatial_name.c_str(), Dims4(N, 22, H, W));
    exec_ctx->setInputShape(I.input_global_name.c_str(),  Dims2(N, 19));
    exec_ctx->setTensorAddress(I.input_spatial_name.c_str(), I.d_input_spatial);
    exec_ctx->setTensorAddress(I.input_global_name.c_str(),  I.d_input_global);
} else {
    // existing MiniGo upload + setInputShape + setTensorAddress
}

// Output binding: identical for both formats — same names, same shapes.
exec_ctx->setTensorAddress(I.policy_name.c_str(),    I.d_policy);
exec_ctx->setTensorAddress(I.value_name.c_str(),     I.d_value);
if (!I.score_name.empty())
    exec_ctx->setTensorAddress(I.score_name.c_str(), I.d_score);
// ... etc, same as MiniGo

// enqueueV3 + read-back + NNOutput packing: identical for both formats.
```

Details:
- Because we baked post-processing into the ONNX graph (phase 0),
  there's no special unpacking math here. `d_value` already holds
  P(W) − P(L) for both formats. `d_score` already holds raw points.
  `d_ownership` already holds [0, 1] sigmoid-equivalent values.
- The optimization profile in `build_or_load_engine` currently calls
  `setDimensions` for input 0 only. Extend it: when there are two
  inputs (detected via `network->getNbInputs() == 2`), set min/opt/
  max dims for both. ~10 LoC at the end of the existing profile
  block.
- Engine cache key already includes the model path, so KataGo and
  MiniGo engines never collide. No cache-key changes needed.

`include/tensorrt_compute.h`: no public API change. All KataGo state
lives in the `Impl` struct.

Test (phase 4 alone): point `main_benchmark` at the KataGo ONNX with
a hand-crafted 9×9 starting board. Compare `policy[0..81]`,
`value`, `score_mean` against the same ONNX run through
ONNX Runtime (already validated in phase 0). Expect agreement within
FP16/BF16 noise (~1e-3 absolute on policy logits, ~1e-3 on value).
If TRT diverges from ORT, the bug is in TRT precision config or
input upload, not the model.

### Phase 5: integration + main_play / main_evaluate

At this point a KataGo ONNX should run end-to-end. Verify (CLI flags
match the actual binaries — see `main_*.cpp` argv parsing):

1. `build/main_play --model kata1.onnx --sims 800 --komi 7.0`
   — interactive GTP-ish play. Check that legal moves are selected
   (rule out off-by-one in policy slot ordering), passes happen at
   end of game, and reported value tracks the expected sign.

2. `build/main_benchmark --model kata1.onnx --max-batch 256
   --nn-iters 1000 --games 4` — measure throughput. Section 5
   (self-play) is skipped automatically for KataGo (see Phase 6
   benchmark guard); sections 1-4 run normally. Expect ≥ 70% of
   MiniGo's b10c128 rate on the same hardware.

3. `build/main_evaluate --model1 kata1.onnx --model2
   minigo_v123.onnx --games 50` — match. The whole point. Both nets
   in one process, same MCTS, same search-thread count, same
   batching. Asymmetric only in the format-dispatch branch.

If any of these fail, the failure mode tells you which phase to
revisit:
- Crashes inside TRT setup → phase 4 (binding / naming).
- Garbage policy / value → phase 3 (encoder bug).
- Loads but says "format not detected" → phase 1 (loader regex).
- Slow → phase 4 (extra cudaMemcpy or under-batched).

### Phase 6: hardening + boundary enforcement

This phase is where we enforce the core principle (KataGo is
inference-only) at every entry point that should reject it.

**Selfplay rejection (P2 review finding).** Add an early guard at the
top of `src/main_selfplay.cpp`, immediately after `LoadedModel::load()`:

```cpp
auto model = LoadedModel::load(model_path);
if (model->format == ModelFormat::KataGo) {
    std::cerr << "ERROR: main_selfplay does not support KataGo-format models.\n"
              << "  KataGo weights are inference-only in this build.\n"
              << "  Use main_play or main_evaluate for KataGo runs.\n";
    return 2;
}
```

~6 LoC. Selfplay's encoder dispatch, augmentation
(`mcts.cpp:608-668`), and V2 record writer remain completely
untouched — the guard prevents any KataGo state from ever reaching
them. **Note:** `main_selfplay.cpp` is *not* in the encoder-rename
list from Phase 2 — its `game.encode()` calls stay exactly as they
are, because the guard ensures they only ever run on MiniGo models.

**Benchmark partial rejection (P1 review finding).**
`src/main_benchmark.cpp` runs five sections: 1-4 measure pure NN
inference / MCTS / batched-eval throughput (KataGo-compatible), but
section 5 (`main_benchmark.cpp:234-256`) runs multi-threaded
self-play via `self_play_game(...)` which builds `TrainingRecord`s
and goes through the augmentation path (`mcts.cpp:608-668`). That
augmentation assumes `state.size() == input_channels * board_area`
and would silently truncate or run past KataGo's 19-float global
tail. Don't run section 5 for KataGo:

```cpp
// src/main_benchmark.cpp — at the top of section 5's block (~line 234):
if (model->format == ModelFormat::KataGo) {
    std::cout << "5. Self-play: SKIPPED for KataGo-format model "
              << "(KataGo runs inference only; selfplay generates "
              << "MiniGo training records).\n";
} else {
    /* existing section 5 body unchanged */
}
```

~5 LoC of `if/else` wrapping. Sections 1-4 (the parts users
actually want for KataGo throughput measurement) keep working.
The encode-dispatch rename in `main_benchmark.cpp` from Phase 2
still applies to the eval path used by sections 1-4.

**Backend rejection (other backends).** Eigen / CUDA / OpenCL /
Metal / RKNN all need an early check in `create_handle` that errors
cleanly if `model->format == ModelFormat::KataGo`:

```cpp
if (model->format == ModelFormat::KataGo)
    throw std::runtime_error(
        "KataGo format requires the TensorRT backend. "
        "Rebuild with `cmake -DMINIGO_BACKEND=tensorrt`.");
```

~4 LoC per backend × 5 backends = ~20 LoC. No other change to those
backends.

**Komi plumbing.** `--komi` from `main_play` / `main_evaluate` /
`main_benchmark` flags into `Config::komi` and through to the
encoder. Currently config has `komi = 6.5f`; KataGo's most common
9×9 setting is `7.0` or `7.5`. Mismatch in komi gives the wrong
global feature and silently degrades strength.

**Rules globals.** Support at least Tromp-Taylor area scoring with
half-komi (our default) and KataGo-default rules (Tromp-Taylor
area, no encore, suicide=disallowed). Encore phases remain
unsupported (the V7 encoder fills those rule-flag globals with
zeros).

**Documentation.** Add `KATAGO_INFERENCE.md` describing:
- The principle: KataGo weights drive inference only.
- The converter workflow (`tools/katago_to_onnx.py`).
- The supported binaries (`main_play`, `main_benchmark`,
  `main_evaluate`) and the rejected ones (`main_selfplay`,
  Python training pipeline).
- Supported rules and known limitations.

`README.md`: short pointer at `KATAGO_INFERENCE.md`. No prose
duplication.


## Verification milestones

Sanity checkpoints during implementation, in order:

**Phase 0 done**:
- `tools/katago_to_onnx.py --katago-bin kata1-b10c128-...bin.gz
  --board 19 --output models/kata1.onnx` produces a valid ONNX file.
- `onnx.checker.check_model("models/kata1.onnx")` passes.
- `tools/katago_parity_test.py` passes: outputs match upstream KataGo
  to within ~1e-3 on at least one reference position (or
  self-consistency check passes if upstream isn't installed).
- **Warm-init regression test** (mandatory): run warm-init on the
  same `.bin.gz` before *and* after the parser-extension change in
  Phase 0.1; assert every tensor in the resulting `training.pt` is
  bit-equal. The parser change is strictly additive only if this
  passes.

**Phase 1 done**:
- `LoadedModel::load()` on `kata1.onnx` prints `type=katago board=9
  channels=22+19`.
- `LoadedModel::load()` on an existing MiniGo ONNX still prints the
  identical line it printed before this change.

**Phase 3 done**:
- Unit test `test_katago_inputs` passes for ≥ 5 reference positions
  (empty, mid-game, ladder, ko, near-endgame). Each plane matches
  upstream byte-for-byte.

**Phase 4 done**:
- `main_benchmark` reports throughput for the KataGo ONNX. Numerical
  outputs match KataGo's own benchmark on the same position to
  within ~1e-3.

**Phase 5 done**:
- `main_evaluate kata1 minigo_v123 --games 200` finishes, prints a
  win-rate. Direction sanity: kata1 should crush a randomly-init or
  early MiniGo. If we ever beat kata1 with a small MiniGo, suspect
  bug, not strength.

**Phase 6 done**:
- README updated. Wrong komi reported as a clear error, not silent
  strength degradation. Wrong backend errors clearly.


## Risks

1. **KataGo architecture port has subtle bugs.** Pre-act vs post-act
   BN, gpool channel split (96+32 of 128), policy head's gpool
   injection into pass logit, value head's pool order (mean+max+std)
   are easy to get wrong. Mitigation: phase 0 parity test against
   upstream KataGo (`tools/katago_parity_test.py`). If `katago` is on
   PATH, this is a hard check; if not, it's a softer consistency
   check. Until parity passes on at least one reference position, do
   not start phase 1.
2. **V7 encoder bugs are silent.** A wrong plane permutation or a
   wrong liberty count gives wrong policy/value but doesn't crash.
   Mitigation: byte-for-byte regression tests against upstream
   reference outputs (phase 3). Also: log a warning if the empty-board
   policy doesn't put the highest probability near the center
   3-3 / 4-4 points (a standard sanity check for any Go net).
3. **Pass-alive and ladder are subtle.** Both are easy to almost-port
   and miss edge cases. Mitigation: copy the algorithm structure
   verbatim from KataGo's source rather than paraphrasing it. Extra
   eyes on these two helpers in code review.
4. **KataGo binary format version drift.** `warm_init_from_katago.py`
   targets versions v8-v15. If kata1 ships v16+, the parser needs
   updating. Mitigation: the parser already prints a warning for
   newer versions and proceeds optimistically; track upstream when
   needed.
5. **Parser extension in `warm_init_from_katago.py` could regress
   warm-init.** The plan now requires retaining the previously-
   discarded head layers to feed `katago_arch.py` (P1 review finding).
   This means `warm_init_from_katago.py` is no longer byte-for-byte
   unchanged — we add `Optional` fields to dataclasses and populate
   them in the parsers. Mitigation: the change is strictly additive
   (required fields are unchanged, new fields default to None,
   warm-init code reaches into none of them). Phase 0.1 mandates a
   byte-diff of `training.pt` before vs. after the parser extension
   on the same `.bin.gz` input. If any tensor differs, do not
   continue — the change is not actually additive.
6. **TRT engine build time on large boards.** kata1 b10c128 at 19×19
   builds in ~30-90 s per (GPU, batch, precision) combo on a 4090.
   Fine for one-off; engine cache (already in place) handles
   subsequent runs. Document in README.
7. **No upstream-export fallback documented.** We deliberately don't
   support running upstream-exported KataGo ONNX files (different
   I/O names + shapes). If our converter has a bug on some kata1
   variant, the user is stuck until we fix it. Mitigation: the
   parity test must pass on the canonical kata1 b10c128 before we
   ship; ongoing kata-version compatibility is a follow-up.


